<</AP<</N 6953 0 R >>/Border[ 0 0 0]/Contents(A stack smash is a type of stack buffer overflow that occurs when an attacker is able to overwrite \r\nthe saved instruction pointer and execute arbitrary code by overflowing a buffer on the stack.\r\nA stack off-by-one is a type of stack overflow that occurs when a program writes one byte of \r\ndata past the end of a buffer on the stack. This can cause the data to overwrite the saved \r\ninstruction pointer and allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code.\r\nA stack canary is a security mechanism that is used to detect stack buffer overflows and prevent \r\nthem from being exploited. It involves placing a unique value, known as a "canary," on the stack \r\nbefore the saved instruction pointer. If an attacker attempts to overflow the buffer and overwrite \r\nthe saved instruction pointer, they will also overwrite the canary. This can be detected and used \r\nto prevent the attack from succeeding.)/DA(/Helv 10.5 Tf 0 Tc 11.76 TL 0 0 0 rg )/F 4/PDFIUM_HasGeneratedAP true/Rect[ 154.35 315.6 606.6 468.6]/Rotate 0/Subtype/FreeText/Type/Annot>>
endobj
